  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.number_of_spanning_trees - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.number_of_spanning_trees.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.number_of_spanning_trees()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Count the spanning trees of the graph (Kirchhoff's matrix-tree theorem).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.number_of_spanning_trees()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Count the spanning trees of the graph (Kirchhoff's matrix-tree theorem).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.number_of_spanning_trees()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Count the spanning trees of the graph (Kirchhoff's matrix-tree theorem).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCount the spanning trees of the graph (Kirchhoff's matrix-tree theorem).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L80870-L81019){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.number_of_spanning_trees(\n    root=None,\n    weighted=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Number of spanning trees](../assets/graphs/number-of-spanning-trees.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a square A, B, C, D with the diagonal A–C in the accent color. Right: its 8 spanning trees as miniatures in two rows. The top row holds the 4 trees of the plain square, each dropping one side; the bottom row holds the 4 trees that use the diagonal. The heading gives the count, 8, and a note under the rows the sum 4 + 4 = 8.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA spanning tree connects all `n` nodes with `n - 1` edges and no cycle. Counting them\nmeasures redundancy: a tree has exactly one spanning tree (lose any edge and it falls\napart), while a complete graph on `n` nodes has $n^{n-2}$ (Cayley's formula). The count\nis used as a reliability measure for communication networks, in statistical physics\nand in enumerative combinatorics, and it can be huge even for small graphs.\n\nOn a directed graph, the method counts spanning arborescences rooted at `root=`: trees\nin which every node can be reached from the root along edge directions. On a weighted\ngraph (or with `weighted=True`), each tree counts as the product of its edge weights,\nwhich gives the normalizing constant for sampling trees in proportion to their weight\n(see `random_spanning_tree()`); pass `weighted=False` for a plain count.\n\nParallel edges count as distinct (each choice gives a different tree) and self-loops\nare ignored. A disconnected graph has no spanning tree, so the result is `0`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[root]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Required on a directed graph, where it is the root of the arborescences (as an\n    integer ID or string label). Ignored on undirected graphs.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to weight each tree by the product of its edge weights. If `None` (default),\n    follows the graph's `weighted` flag.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [int or float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Unweighted counts are exact integers, however large. Weighted counts are floats.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is empty, or `root=` is missing or unknown on a directed graph.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nBy Kirchhoff's theorem the number of spanning trees equals any cofactor of the Laplacian\n$L = D - A$: delete one row and the matching column and take the determinant. For\narborescences rooted at $r$ (Tutte's directed version) the in-degree Laplacian is used\nand the row and column of $r$ are deleted. Unweighted determinants are computed\nexactly: with Bareiss' fraction-free elimination in 128-bit integers when the\nintermediate values fit, and otherwise modulo enough 61-bit primes to exceed Hadamard's\nbound on the determinant, combined with the Chinese remainder theorem. Each pass costs\n$O(V^3)$ operations. Weighted determinants use a floating-point LU decomposition.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~all_spanning_trees`) \n\n:   List the spanning trees.\n\n[](`~random_spanning_tree`) \n\n:   Draw a spanning tree at random.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA 4-cycle has four spanning trees, one for each edge left out, and a diagonal raises the\ncount to eight:\n\n::: {#a2d6aede .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nsquare = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"A\")]\n)\nbefore = square.number_of_spanning_trees()\n\nsquare.add_edge(\"A\", \"C\")\nbefore, square.number_of_spanning_trees()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(4, 8)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOn a directed graph, the count is of the trees reached from `root=`. Rooted at `A`, this\ngraph has two, but rooted at `B` it has only one:\n\n::: {#b0d1765e .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"A\", \"C\")], directed=True\n)\n\n(\n    graph.number_of_spanning_trees(root=\"A\"),\n    graph.number_of_spanning_trees(root=\"B\"),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(2, 1)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### How many tracks the network can lose\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network. Its 25 tracks hold 3828 spanning trees, so\nthere are many ways to keep every station reachable while tracks are closed. Since the\nnetwork is weighted by minutes, `weighted=False` asks for the plain count:\n\n::: {#7f0f7a41 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\ntotal = metro.number_of_spanning_trees(weighted=False)\ntotal\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n3828\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nClosing a track removes every tree that uses it. The share of trees that use each track\nis 1 for the five tracks that every route depends on, and 0.47 for the track between\nCentral and Riverside, which has the most alternatives (drawn lightest):\n\n::: {#ac8699f6 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nshare = {}\nfor edge in metro.get_edge_ids():\n    closed = ec.metro().delete_edge(id=edge)\n    left = closed.number_of_spanning_trees(weighted=False)\n    share[edge] = round(1 - left / total, 2)\n\nmetro.set_edge_attrs(\"share\", share)\nmetro.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", edge_color_by=\"share\", edge_width=4, edge_opacity=1,\n    label_by=\"label\",\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-0cbef270:#73a2da;--ect-2dd6b114:#335d94;--ect-1e984f1d:#81b1e9;--ect-5dd4fa0a:#5683bb;--ect-7bcf0c07:#3f69a1;--ect-c36c77f1:#75a4dd;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-975840b8:#184f95;--ect-f8094ac6:#2c5d9f;--ect-472663e1:#3d6caa;--ect-6826a5f9:#4f7ab4;--ect-9d43aea2:#6089be;--ect-5ee57d66:#7297c9;--ect-3c980f0e:#84a6d3;--ect-ec386ef0:#96b5dd;--ect-97f76ec5:#a8c4e7;--ect-e28c6970:#bad3f1;--ect-6425850a:#cde2fb;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-0cbef270:#3565a5;--ect-2dd6b114:#92b2db;--ect-1e984f1d:#205599;--ect-5dd4fa0a:#5d86bc;--ect-7bcf0c07:#80a3d1;--ect-c36c77f1:#3162a3;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--